::heading level=1 id=final-validation
Release Gates
::end

::paragraph id=final-validation-summary
What has to be green before a change ships, and the command that says so. Each gate is a command anyone can run, not a claim: a status here is only worth what re-running it proves.
::end

::html id=final-validation-table
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Command</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>Tests</td><td><code>cd rust &amp;&amp; cargo test</code></td><td>Every crate, including the byte-exact format fixtures.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Lints</td><td><code>cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings</code></td><td>No warning survives to the next reader.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Formatting</td><td><code>cargo fmt --check</code></td><td>The tree is in one shape.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Extension</td><td><code>node --test "editor/github/test/*.test.mjs"</code></td><td>Both wasm builds render what the binary renders.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Documents</td><td><code>dx fmt examples/*.dx documents/*.dx --check</code></td><td>The corpus is canonical, so a save never rewrites it.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
::end

::heading level=2 id=final-validation-invariants-heading
What the gates are protecting
::end

::bulleted-list id=final-validation-invariants
- Storage cannot lose a byte. A chunk holds the exact canonical text of one block.
- Canonical output must not drift, and a fixture is captured output — never scripture.
- Reading never executes and never writes. Only `dx run` runs anything.
- Untrusted input is everything a document carries; `render::escape` stays an allow-list.
- One engine. The editor, the CLI, and github.com render through `doc-core` or not at all.
::end

::heading level=2 id=final-validation-order-heading
The order they run in
::end

::svg id=final-validation-gauge
<svg viewBox="0 0 720 120" role="img" aria-label="Gates run in order: tests, then lints, then formatting, then the extension suite, then the document corpus">
	<g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-opacity="0.45">
		<line x1="60" y1="46" x2="660" y2="46"/>
		<circle cx="60" cy="46" r="5"/>
		<circle cx="210" cy="46" r="5"/>
		<circle cx="360" cy="46" r="5"/>
		<circle cx="510" cy="46" r="5"/>
		<circle cx="660" cy="46" r="5"/>
	</g>
	<g fill="currentColor" font-family="monospace" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">
		<text x="60" y="30">tests</text>
		<text x="210" y="30">lints</text>
		<text x="360" y="30">fmt</text>
		<text x="510" y="30">extension</text>
		<text x="660" y="30">documents</text>
	</g>
	<g fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-family="monospace" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">
		<text x="60" y="72">correctness</text>
		<text x="210" y="72">cleanliness</text>
		<text x="360" y="72">shape</text>
		<text x="510" y="72">parity</text>
		<text x="660" y="72">canonical</text>
	</g>
	<text x="360" y="106" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55" font-family="monospace" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">a failure stops the line — nothing downstream is meaningful yet</text>
</svg>
::end

::quote id=final-validation-note
No success claim without validation. When you fix a bug, add the test that would have caught it.
::end
